    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "\"John S. Newberry, as geologist and paleontologist, accompanied Capt. Macomb's 1859 survey party from Santa Fe, New Mexico to the confluence of the Green and Grand Fork of the Colorado River. U.S. territories were only generally mapped, and natural resources known in general terms at that time. The population was moving westward rapidly. The California gold rush accelerated the need for safe transcontinental navigation. Four great railroad surveys were commissioned to determine the route of the west coast first rail link. Boundary Surveys undertook to define and refine the southern, western, and northern boundaries. The Colorado River was recognized to be an extremely valuable asset for irrigation, drinking water, and potentially navigation for the southwestern region. The total length of the Colorado River was not known and its route had not been charted. Period maps were rough; for example, the Colorado River confluence with the Green River, its major tributary, was incorrectly positioned in Arizona on some American maps. The Old Spanish Trail map by Father Escalante in the sixteenth century told quite a different story. It included tribal lands, available waters as well as the generally correct regional geography. Macomb charted his outbound route based primarily on this deserted trail.\"",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
